## Why This Is Asked

Candidates must handle time-decayed scoring with dynamic insert/erase and frequent top-k queries over a moving window, a realistic pattern in recommender and CDN analytics at large scale.

## Key Concepts

- Time-decayed scoring and exponential decay
- Windowed top-k queries with lazy deletions
- Maintaining running sums in a sliding window
- Trade-offs: lazy vs eager pruning, memory vs accuracy

## Code Example

```javascript
class TimeDecayStore {
  constructor(lambda) { 
    this.lambda = lambda; 
    this.map = new Map(); 
    this.heap = new MaxHeap(); 
    this.currentT = 0; 
  }

  insert(id, score, t) {
    this.map.set(id, { score, t_last: t });
    this.heap.insert({ id, decayedScore: score, t });
  }

  update(id, score, t) {
    this.map.set(id, { score, t_last: t });
    this.heap.insert({ id, decayedScore: score, t });
  }

  erase(id) {
    this.map.delete(id);
  }

  topKInLastWindow(k, t, W) {
    this.currentT = t;
    this.pruneExpired(t - W);
    const results = [];
    const temp = [];

    while (results.length < k && !this.heap.isEmpty()) {
      const item = this.heap.extractMax();
      const current = this.map.get(item.id);

      if (current) {
        const decayed = current.score * Math.exp(-this.lambda * (t - current.t_last));
        if (current.t_last >= t - W) {
          results.push({ id: item.id, decayedScore: decayed });
          temp.push({ id: item.id, decayedScore: decayed, t: current.t_last });
        }
      }
    }

    temp.forEach(item => this.heap.insert(item));
    return results;
  }

  sumScoresInWindow(t, W) {
    let sum = 0;
    for (const [id, { score, t_last }] of this.map) {
      if (t_last >= t - W) {
        sum += score * Math.exp(-this.lambda * (t - t_last));
      }
    }
    return sum;
  }

  pruneExpired(windowStart) {
    // Lazy pruning during queries
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of practical queue augmentations and min-tracking with guaranteed O(1) per operation.

## Key Concepts
- Min-tracking with stack-based queues
- Amortized analysis of transfers between stacks
- Edge-case handling for empty states and duplicates

## Code Example
```javascript
class MinQueue {
  constructor(){ this.inbox = []; this.outbox = [] }
  _push(stack, val){ const m = stack.length ? Math.min(val, stack[stack.length - 1].min) : val; stack.push({ val, min: m }) }
  enqueue(x){ this._push(this.inbox, x) }
  dequeue(){ if (!this.outbox.length){ while (this.inbox.length){ const el = this.inbox.pop(); const m = this.outbox.length ? Math.min(el.val, this.outbox[this.outbox.length - 1].min) : el.val; this.outbox.push({ val: el.val, min: m }) } }
    return this.outbox.pop().val }
  getMin(){ const mins = []; if (this.inbox.length) mins.push(this.inbox[this.inbox.length - 1].min); if (this.outbox.length) mins.push(this.outbox[this.outbox.length - 1].min); return mins.length ? Math.min(...mins) : undefined }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Maintains compact histogram with dynamic quantile queries in O(log D).

## Key Concepts
- Fenwick tree for prefix counts over fixed domain
- Lower_bound on Fenwick to locate quantile index
- Edge handling when total is zero or p at bounds

## Code Example
```javascript
class HistogramQuantile {
  constructor(){
    this.N = 2001;
    this.ft = new Array(this.N+1).fill(0);
  }
  update(i, delta){
    for (let x = i; x <= this.N; x += x & -x) this.ft[x] += delta;
  }
  insertValue(v){ this.update(v + 1001, 1); }
  eraseValue(v){ this.update(v + 1001, -1); }
  sum(i){ let s = 0; for (let x = i; x > 0; x -= x & -x) s += this.ft[x]; return s; }
  lowerBound(k){
    let idx = 0, bit = 1 << 11; // > 2001
    let acc = 0;
    for (; bit; bit >>= 1){
      const next = idx + bit;
      if (next <= this.N && acc + this.ft[next] < k){ idx = next; acc += this.ft[next]; }
    }
    return idx + 1;
  }
  findQuantile(p){
    const total = this.sum(this.N);
    if (total === 0) return null;
    const k = Math.ceil(p * total);
    const i = this.lowerBound(k);
    return i - 1001;
  }
}
```

